  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 245 AM Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A Heat Advisory remains in effect today for parts \nof the Triangle and south-central NC, and we've issued another Heat \nAdvisory for the Triangle to the S and E for Mon. The hot weather \nwill continue into mid week, will gradually lowering heat indices \nmid through late week. \n\nHeating has been curbed somewhat today by this morning's areas of \nlow clouds as well as the early-onset of convection across parts of \nthe Sandhills and far N CWA. But several locations have still \nmanaged to reach a 105F heat index today, as pockets of strong \nheating combine with high dewpoints in the mid-upper 70s. We should \nsee a bit less cloudiness Mon morning, and with low level \nthicknesses expected to reach 20 m above normal, highs should reach \nthe mid 90s to near 100F. The small bit of good news is that the \ndewpoints tomorrow are expected to be slightly lower than today, \ndipping into the 70-76 range with mixing in the afternoon, with \nthese lower readings in the west, and this should help keep heat \nindices from reaching criteria for an Extreme Heat Warning, although \ncertainly this will be assessed further tonight and monitored \nclosely on Mon. But a Heat Advisory is warranted for all but NW and \nVA border portions of the CWA, with heat index values expected to \nreach 105-109F. NWS Heat Risk, which is based on how unusual and/or \nlong-lived this heat is, as well as on past CDC heat-illness data, \nreaches a level 3 (out of 4) nearly everywhere Mon, suggesting that \nthis heat could be particularly dangerous. \n\nTemps may be a bit less hot on Tue, but still elevated over the S \nand E CWA, where the long duration of this hot stretch will add to \nthe already-high heat stress. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... The threat for a few strong to severe storms \ncontinues through Mon. We have a Marginal (level 1 of 5) risk of \nsevere thunderstorms across the northern half of central NC for the \nrest of today, and a Slight (level 2 of 5) risk in the Piedmont on \nMon. Damaging winds are the primary threat.\n\nA few strong to severe storms remain possible today and Mon. Deep \nlayer bulk shear remains poor today, under 20 kts, which will limit \nthe intensity and duration of any updraft. But SBCAPE has reached \n2500-4000 J/kg thus far (according to RAP-based mesoanalysis) with a \nDCAPE over 800 J/kg much of the area, highest SW, and with PWs still \naround 2\" or up to 130%-140% of normal, isolated water-loaded \ndowndrafts remain possible, leading to localized strong to severe \ngusts into early this evening. On Mon, the SBCAPE may be a bit \nlower, but our deep layer shear is expected to be around 25 kts, \nwith added forcing for ascent provided by an approaching MCV \nexpected later in the day from the NW. In particular, convection \nover the NC mountains may maintain strength with better organization \nas they track ESE into the NC Piedmont, again with damaging winds as \nthe primary threat, from mid afternoon through mid evening. \n\nStorm coverage is expected to decrease and push to our SE sections \non Tue, as a slug of drier air with lower PW spreads in from the WNW.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... Rain and thunderstorm chances will increase from \nlate Thursday through the weekend as a slow-moving cold front stalls \nover the region, though exact timing and impacts remain uncertain.\n\nAs we head into late week, a stronger cold front is expected to move \ninto the region. Forecast guidance shows precipitation chances \nincreasing late Thursday ahead of a slow moving trough. The frontal \nboundary is expected to stall over the region before eventually \nwashing out. This lingering boundary will act as a focus for daily \nrain and thunderstorm chances returning Friday afternoon/evening and \npersisting through the weekend. Given how far out this is, there are \nstill several details to look into. Will closely monitor forecast \ntrends over the coming days for any adjustments in the timing, \namounts, and exact placement of this frontal boundary which will \nthen result in a better idea for how isolated or widespread precip \nchances will be over the weekend.",
